+//!    nested paging) and must exit on HLT with AX = 0x1234. Proves EFER.SVME, the host save area,
+//!    the VMCB layout, VMRUN and #VMEXIT.
+//! 2. `test_nested_paging`: a 32-bit paged guest gets its own physical address space through
+//!    nested page tables. It writes 0x1234 to its address 0x100 (the host must find it in the
+//!    backing frame),
+//!    then reads unmapped address 0x5000, which must exit with a nested page fault for 0x5000. The
+//!    host maps a page holding 0xBEEF there and resumes; the guest must finish with AX = 0xBEEF.
+//!    Proves NPT, NPF exits and resuming a guest after an exit (demand mapping).
 //!
-//! ponytail: three frames are leaked (VMCB, host save area, guest code) and SVM stays enabled in
-//! EFER after the test; fine for a one-shot boot self-test, replace with a real vCPU lifecycle
-//! once there is a /scheme/vmm.
+//! VMCB offsets follow FreeBSD's sys/amd64/vmm/amd/vmcb.h (bhyve, BSD-2-Clause) and the AMD APM
